<code>assert (e == null) => this.lastElement() == null : (e != null) => this.lastElement().equals(e);</code>

However, the above assert statement uses a ternary operator which is not valid in JML (Java Modeling Language) syntax. Instead, we should use logical operators to express the condition. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert (e == null) ==> this.lastElement() == null && (e != null) ==> this.lastElement().equals(e);</code>

This ensures that the assertion correctly reflects the conditions specified in the comment. Let's wrap it in the required `<code>` block:

<code>assert (e == null) ==> this.lastElement() == null && (e != null) ==> this.lastElement().equals(e);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert (e == null) => (this.lastElement() == null) && (e != null) => (this.lastElement().equals(e));</code>
--------------------
